  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Flash flood potential has decreased but there \nis still a marginal risk for localized heavy rain today.\n\nAn anomalous slow moving upper level low centered just to our\nwest will continue to plague the forecast area with broad  \nsynoptic ascent impinging into an abundance of moisture laden \nair over the eastern CONUS. Regional 00Z U/A regional soundings\nmeasured PWATs from 1.6 to 2.0 inches, down slightly from\nyesterdays 12Z numbers but still around the 90th percentile\ncompared to normal. This environment remains ripe for for heavy\nrain, promoting high rainfall rates and efficiency.\n\nArea to watch today will be surface front associated with the\nupper trough, this front becoming aligned with the Blue Ridge\nthis afternoon. As such, think this will be the place to watch\nfor thunderstorm development during the afternoon with\nopportunity for some repeat rainfall over the same areas of\nwhich received heavy rain Saturday night within Patrick and \nSurry Counties. Both the NamNest and RRFS suggest storm cells to\ndevelop vctny of Meadows of Dan or between Hillsville and \nMartinsville along the Hwy 58 Corridor...and along the southern \nBlue Ridge into NC. Other storm cells are expected initiate \nalong the front as it moves east of CHO-LYH, the front moving \nfarther east along its northern extent that it does along the \nsouthern Blue Ridge.\n\nAttm will hold off on an areal flood watch and wait to see where\nactual initiation takes place before doing a more targeted \nheadline. We also, in coordination with public officials, have a\nflood watch for some of the dams within the headwaters of the \nArarat River given the inundation that took place there over the\nweekend (see hydro section of this AFD for details). \n\nThe actual surface front should move east of the mountains\ntonight, models suggesting winds shifting to the north this\nevening, then from a northeasterly direction overnight. The\nnortheast wind may result in additional convergence as a shallow\nwedge develops east of the Blue Ridge overnight yet still\nencountering some southerly flow aloft that will be overrunning\nthe front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues through much of the\nweek.\n \nThe closed low is forecast to gradually settle southeast over\nthe southern Appalachians tonight and Tuesday before weakening \nand shearing out mid week. Models suggest that Tuesday should \nbe a quieter day per stable wedge but then upslope southeast \nwind flow develops Tuesday night, with rain redeveloping from \nsouth to north across the forecast area, maintaining opportunity\nfor showers and thunderstorms with marginal risk of excessive \nrainfall. After mid-week, we still have daily afternoon and \nevening thundershower chances, but no real change in airmass. In\ngeneral weather will stay moist with surface dewpoints flirting\nwith 70 the entire week.",
